.\" Render with: groff -man -Tutf8 smithproxy.1
.TH SMITHPROXY 1 "OCT 2026" "Smithproxy" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
smithproxy \- policy-driven TCP, UDP and TLS inspection proxy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smithproxy
.RB [ \-D ]
.RB [ \-c " file" ]
.RB [ \-o ]
.RB [ \-\-debug | \-\-diagnose | \-\-dump | \-\-extreme ]
.PP
.B smithproxy
.RB [ \-h | \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Smithproxy is a Linux traffic proxy and inspection service written in C++17.
It accepts transparent TPROXY and REDIRECT traffic as well as explicit SOCKS4,
SOCKS5 and HTTP CONNECT clients. TCP, UDP and TLS connections are matched
against ordered policies which select routing, TLS, DNS, content and detection
profiles.
.PP
TLS inspection terminates the client-side connection and creates a separate
upstream connection. Generated server certificates are signed by a configured
Smithproxy CA. Clients must trust that CA when TLS interception is enabled.
.PP
Smithproxy is normally started by the system service. Runtime configuration,
diagnostics, session inspection and configuration editing are available through
the interactive CLI.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c " file, " \-\-config-file " file"
Load the specified configuration file instead of
.IR /etc/smithproxy/smithproxy.cfg .
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-config-check-only
Load and validate the configuration, print diagnostics and exit without
starting proxy listeners.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-daemonize
Fork into the background. File logging must be configured. A service manager
is preferred for normal deployments.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Enable debug-level startup logging.
.TP
.B \-\-diagnose
Enable diagnostic startup logging.
.TP
.B \-\-dump
Enable verbose dump-level startup logging.
.TP
.B \-\-extreme
Enable the most verbose startup logging.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the Smithproxy and Socle versions, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print command-line help and exit.
.SH TRAFFIC MODES
.TP
.B Transparent routed traffic
Linux TPROXY rules divert routed TCP, UDP and TLS traffic to Smithproxy while
preserving the original destination. Policies may also preserve the original
source address on the upstream connection when routing permits it.
.TP
.B Locally originated traffic
REDIRECT rules can divert traffic created on the Smithproxy host. Original UDP
destinations are not available through this mode; local UDP handling is
therefore primarily intended for DNS.
.TP
.B Explicit proxy
SOCKS4/SOCKS5 and HTTP CONNECT listeners accept explicit client connections.
Hostnames supplied by the client are resolved by Smithproxy and pass through
the same policy and inspection path as transparent traffic.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The main configuration is a libconfig file. The default path is:
.nf
    /etc/smithproxy/smithproxy.cfg
.fi
.PP
Most settings can be inspected and edited in the interactive CLI. Use
.B save config
to persist the active configuration. Smithproxy can create a backup and upgrade
older configuration schemas while loading them; review service logs after an
upgrade.
.PP
Network interception itself is prepared by the installed startup scripts and
their settings in:
.nf
    /etc/smithproxy/smithproxy.startup.cfg
.fi
.SH INTERACTIVE CLI
Run
.B sx_cli
to connect to the local command server. The wrapper reads the configured CLI
port and uses a telnet client:
.nf
    sx_cli
.fi
.PP
The CLI supports command completion, contextual help, configuration modes,
runtime diagnostics and privilege levels. The default base port is 50000 when
the wrapper cannot read one from the configuration.
.SS Prompt
With an active color palette, the prompt uses U+2300 DIAMETER SIGN as a
terminal-sized form of the Smithproxy mark:
.nf
    smithpr\[u2300]xy(hostname)>
    smithpr\[u2300]xy(hostname)#
.fi
.PP
The hash prompt indicates privileged mode. Configuration mode, unsaved changes
and configuration-load errors are appended when applicable. With colors
disabled, the prompt uses the ASCII form:
.nf
    smithproxy(hostname)>
.fi
.SS Terminal colors
ANSI styling and the palette are controlled per CLI session:
.nf
    terminal color auto [style]
    terminal color on [style]
    terminal color off
.fi
.PP
.B auto
uses the capability selected by the CLI frontend,
.B on
forces ANSI styling, and
.B off
removes ANSI sequences and the Unicode prompt mark. Available styles are:
.nf
    classic solarized monokai nord gruvbox
    matrix monochrome amber ice
.fi
.PP
Use
.B terminal color off
with older telnet clients, missing terminal fonts, log capture or machine
parsing. Modern UTF-8 terminals and xterm.js display the branded prompt.
.SH CAPTURE AND OBSERVABILITY
Policies and content profiles can write per-connection SMCap or PCAP files, or
append traffic to a rotating single PCAP. Remote capture export uses GRE and
can carry plaintext SPQ1 packets or native PCAPNG records, depending on the
configured GRE format.
.PP
TLS key material can be written in SSLKEYLOG format. Smithproxy also exposes
live sessions, connection history, performance counters and event diagnostics
through the CLI. Optional webhooks report connection lifecycle events and can
participate in policy decisions. An authenticated HTTP API can expose selected
configuration operations when enabled at build time and configured with a key.
.SH PROTOCOL SUPPORT
Smithproxy handles IPv4 and IPv6 TCP/UDP traffic, TLS and STARTTLS, DNS and DoH,
HTTP/1, HTTP/2, SOCKS4/SOCKS5 and HTTP CONNECT.
.PP
TLS validation features include OCSP, CRL handling and optional Certificate
Transparency checks. SSLKEYLOG and KTLS support depend on the linked OpenSSL
version and build configuration.
.SS QUIC and HTTP/3
Transparent QUIC interception is experimental and disabled by default. Full
listener support requires OpenSSL 3.5 or newer with QUIC server APIs. Enable it
explicitly with the
.B quic_port
and
.B quic_workers
settings and configure the corresponding TPROXY rules. The listener negotiates
HTTP/3, proxies bidirectional QUIC streams and can expose decoded HTTP/3 headers
in native capture output. Inspect active listeners and sessions with:
.nf
    diag proxy quic list
.fi
.PP
The supplied network startup script treats configured QUIC ports separately
from generic UDP. When QUIC interception is disabled, it can block those ports
to prevent HTTP/3 from bypassing the TLS inspection policy.
.SS SSH
SSH can pass through Smithproxy as ordinary TCP traffic. Builds with libssh
support can also perform SSHv2 man-in-the-middle inspection when a policy assigns
an
.B ssh_profile .
The initial implementation supports password-authenticated session channels;
other SSH features may remain opaque or unsupported.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/smithproxy/smithproxy.cfg
Main service configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/smithproxy/smithproxy.startup.cfg
Startup and Linux interception settings.
.TP
.I /etc/smithproxy/users.cfg
Optional authentication configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/smithproxy/certs/default/
Default CA, client and server keys and certificates.
.TP
.I /var/log/smithproxy/
Service logs and optional TLS key log.
.TP
.I /var/smithproxy/data/
Default local capture directory.
.TP
.I /usr/share/smithproxy/
Installed helper modules, tools, documentation and integration files.
.SH SECURITY
Transparent interception normally requires root privileges or equivalent Linux
capabilities and routing configuration. A Smithproxy CA private key permits TLS
impersonation for clients which trust that CA; protect the key and restrict
access to the CLI, API, captures and TLS key logs.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Transparent interception relies on Linux networking facilities. Features such
as HTTP/2, HTTP/3, SSH MITM, KTLS, PAM, the HTTP API and some capture formats
may be conditional on build options or dependency versions. SSH MITM requires
libssh development files at build time. Consult the current online documentation
for scenario-specific constraints.
